## an10.13
an10.13:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are ten fetters. | T: “There are these ten fetters.
an10.13:1.2 | S: What ten? | T: Which ten?
an10.13:1.3 | S: The five lower fetters and the five higher fetters. | T: Five lower fetters & five higher fetters.
an10.13:1.4 | S: What are the five lower fetters? | T: And which are the five lower fetters?
an10.13:1.5 | S: Substantialist view, doubt, misapprehension of precepts and observances, sensual desire, and ill will. | T: Self-identification views, uncertainty, grasping at habits & practices, sensual desire, & ill will.
an10.13:1.6 | S: These are the five lower fetters. | T: These are the five lower fetters.
an10.13:2.1 | S: What are the five higher fetters? | T: And which are the five higher fetters?
an10.13:2.2 | S: Desire for rebirth in the realm of luminous form, desire for rebirth in the formless realm, conceit, restlessness, and ignorance. | T: Passion for form, passion for what is formless, conceit, restlessness, & ignorance.
an10.13:2.3 | S: These are the five higher fetters. | T: These are the five higher fetters.
an10.13:2.4 | S: These are the ten fetters.” | T: And these are the ten fetters.”
an10.13:2.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.15
an10.15:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, the Realized One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha, is said to be the best of all sentient beings—be they footless, with two feet, four feet, or many feet; with form or formless; with perception or without perception or with neither perception nor non-perception. | T: “To the extent that there are animals—footless, two-footed, four-footed, many footed; with form or formless; percipient, non-percipient, or neither percipient nor non-percipient—the Tathāgata, worthy & rightly self-awakened, is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:1.2 | S: In the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in diligence and meet at diligence, and diligence is said to be the best of them. | T: In the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:2.1 | S: The footprints of all creatures that walk can fit inside an elephant’s footprint, so an elephant’s footprint is said to be the biggest of them all. | T: “Just as the footprints of all legged animals are encompassed by the footprint of the elephant, and the elephant’s footprint is reckoned the foremost among them in terms of size;
an10.15:2.2 | S: In the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in diligence and meet at diligence, and diligence is said to be the best of them. | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:3.1 | S: The rafters of a bungalow all lean to the peak, slope to the peak, and meet at the peak, so the peak is said to be the topmost of them all. | T: “Just as the rafters in a peak-roofed house all go to the roof-peak, incline to the roof-peak, converge at the roof-peak, and the roof-peak is reckoned the foremost among them;
an10.15:3.2 | S: In the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in diligence and meet at diligence, and diligence is said to be the best of them. | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:4.1 | S: Of all kinds of fragrant root, spikenard is said to be the best. | T: “Just as, of all root fragrances, black aloes-root is reckoned the foremost;
an10.15:4.2 | S: In the same way … | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:5.1 | S: Of all kinds of fragrant heartwood, red sandalwood is said to be the best. | T: “Just as, of all wood fragrances, red sandalwood is reckoned the foremost;
an10.15:5.2 | S: In the same way … | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:6.1 | S: Of all kinds of fragrant flower, jasmine is said to be the best. | T: “Just as, of all flower fragrances, jasmine is reckoned the foremost;
an10.15:6.2 | S: In the same way … | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:7.1 | S: All lesser kings are vassals of a wheel-turning monarch, so the wheel-turning monarch is said to be the foremost of them all. | T: “Just as all wattle-and-daub-town princes fall subject to a wheel-turning emperor, and the wheel-turning emperor is reckoned the foremost among them;
an10.15:7.2 | S: In the same way … | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:8.1 | S: The radiance of all the stars is not worth a sixteenth part of the moon’s radiance, so the moon’s radiance is said to be the best of them all. | T: “Just as all the light of the constellations does not equal one sixteenth of the light of the moon, and the light of the moon is reckoned the foremost among them;
an10.15:8.2 | S: In the same way … | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:9.1 | S: In the autumn the heavens are sunny and cloudless. And as the sun is rising to the firmament, having dispelled all the darkness of space, it shines and glows and dazzles. | T: “Just as in the last month of the rains, in autumn, when the sky is clear & cloudless, the sun, on ascending the sky, overpowers the space immersed in darkness, shines, blazes, & dazzles;
an10.15:9.2 | S: In the same way … | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.
an10.15:10.1 | S: All the great rivers—that is, the Ganges, Yamunā, Aciravatī, Sarabhū, and Mahī—flow, slant, slope, and incline toward the ocean, and the ocean is said to be the greatest of them. | T: “Just as the great rivers—such as the Ganges, the Yamunā, the Aciravatī, the Sarabhū, & the Mahī—all go to the ocean, incline to the ocean, slope to the ocean, tend toward the ocean, and the ocean is reckoned the foremost among them;
an10.15:10.2 | S: In the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in diligence and meet at diligence, and diligence is said to be the best of them.” | T: in the same way, all skillful qualities are rooted in heedfulness, converge in heedfulness, and heedfulness is reckoned the foremost among them.”
an10.15:10.3 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

