an10.176:2.3 | S: ‘Please, my man, rising early you should stroke the earth from your bed. | T: ‘Come, now, my good man: Get up at the proper time from your bed and touch the earth.
an10.176:2.4 | S: If you don’t stroke the earth, stroke fresh cow dung. | T: If you don’t touch the earth, touch wet cow dung.
an10.176:2.5 | S: If you don’t stroke fresh cow dung, stroke green grass. | T: If you don’t touch wet cow dung, touch green grass.
an10.176:2.6 | S: If you don’t stroke green grass, serve the sacred flame. | T: If you don’t touch green grass, worship a fire.
an10.176:2.7 | S: If you don’t serve the sacred flame, revere the sun with cupped palms. | T: If you don’t worship a fire, pay homage to the sun with clasped hands.
an10.176:2.8 | S: If you don’t revere the sun with cupped palms, immerse yourself in water three times, including at dusk.’ | T: If you don’t pay homage to the sun with clasped hands, go down into the water three times by nightfall.’
an10.176:2.9 | S: The western brahmins advocate this kind of purity.” | T: These are the purification rites declared by the brahmans of the Western lands… of which I approve.”
an10.176:3.1 | S: “The purity advocated by the western brahmins is quite different from that in the training of the Noble One.” | T: “Cunda, the purification rites declared by the brahmans of the Western lands… are one thing; the purification in the discipline of the noble ones is something else entirely.”
an10.176:3.2 | S: “But what, sir, is purity in the training of the Noble One? | T: “But how is there purification in the discipline of the noble ones, venerable sir?
an10.176:3.3 | S: Sir, please teach me this.” | T: It would be good if the Blessed One would teach me how there is purification in the discipline of the noble ones.”
an10.176:4.1 | S: “Well then, brahmin, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then in that case, Cunda, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an10.176:4.2 | S: “Yes, sir,” Cunda replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” Cunda the silversmith responded to him.
an10.176:4.3 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
an10.176:5.1 | S: “Cunda, impurity is threefold by way of body, | T: “There are three ways in which one is made impure by bodily action,
an10.176:5.2 | S: fourfold by way of speech, | T: four ways in which one is made impure by verbal action,
an10.176:5.3 | S: and threefold by way of mind. | T: and three ways in which one is made impure by mental action.
an10.176:6.1 | S: And how is impurity threefold by way of body? | T: Unskillful Bodily Action “And how is one made impure in three ways by bodily action?
an10.176:6.2 | S: It’s when someone kills living creatures. They’re violent, bloody-handed, a hardened killer, merciless to all living beings. | T: There is the case where a certain person takes life, is brutal, bloody-handed, devoted to killing & slaying, showing no mercy to living beings.
an10.176:7.1 | S: They steal. With the intention to commit theft, they take the wealth or belongings of others from village or wilderness. | T: He takes what is not given. He takes, in the manner of a thief, things in a village or a wilderness that belong to others and have not been given by them.
an10.176:8.1 | S: They commit sexual misconduct. They have relations with women who have their mother, father, both mother and father, brother, sister, relatives, or clan as guardian. They have relations with a woman who is protected on principle, or who has a husband, or whose violation is punishable by law, or even one who has been garlanded as a token of betrothal. | T: He engages in sexual misconduct. He gets sexually involved with those who are protected by their mothers, their fathers, their brothers, their sisters, their relatives, or their Dhamma; those with husbands, those who entail punishments, or even those crowned with flowers by another man.
an10.176:9.1 | S: This is the threefold impurity by way of body. | T: This is how one is made impure in three ways by bodily action.
an10.176:10.1 | S: And how is impurity fourfold by way of speech? | T: Unskillful Verbal Action “And how is one made impure in four ways by verbal action?
an10.176:10.2 | S: It’s when someone lies. They’re summoned to a council, an assembly, a family meeting, a guild, or to the royal court, and asked to bear witness: ‘Please, my friend, say what you know.’ Not knowing, they say ‘I know.’ Knowing, they say ‘I don’t know.’ Not seeing, they say ‘I see.’ And seeing, they say ‘I don’t see.’ So they deliberately lie for the sake of themselves or another, or for some trivial worldly reason. | T: There is the case where a certain person tells lies. When he has been called to a town meeting, a group meeting, a gathering of his relatives, his guild, or of the royalty [i.e., a royal court proceeding], if he is asked as a witness, ‘Come & tell, good man, what you know’: If he doesn’t know, he says, ‘I know.’ If he does know, he says, ‘I don’t know.’ If he hasn’t seen, he says, ‘I have seen.’ If he has seen, he says, ’I haven’t seen.’ Thus he consciously tells lies for his own sake, for the sake of another, or for the sake of a certain reward.
an10.176:11.1 | S: They backbite. They repeat in one place what they heard in another so as to divide people against each other. And so they divide those who are harmonious, supporting division, delighting in division, loving division, speaking words that promote division. | T: He engages in divisive speech. What he has heard here he tells there to break those people apart from these people here. What he has heard there he tells here to break these people apart from those people there. Thus breaking apart those who are united and stirring up strife between those who have broken apart, he loves factionalism, delights in factionalism, enjoys factionalism, speaks things that create factionalism.
an10.176:12.1 | S: They speak harshly. They use the kinds of words that are cruel, nasty, hurtful, offensive, bordering on anger, not leading to immersion. | T: He engages in harsh speech. He speaks words that are insolent, cutting, mean to others, reviling others, provoking anger and destroying concentration.
an10.176:13.1 | S: They talk nonsense. Their speech is untimely, and is neither factual nor beneficial. It has nothing to do with the teaching or the training. Their words have no value, and are untimely, unreasonable, rambling, and pointless. | T: He engages in idle chatter. He speaks out of season, speaks what isn’t factual, what isn’t in accordance with the goal, the Dhamma, & the Vinaya, words that are not worth treasuring: unseasonable, unreasonable, uncircumscribed, unconnected with the goal.
an10.176:13.2 | S: This is the fourfold impurity by way of speech. | T: This is how one is made impure in four ways by verbal action.
an10.176:14.1 | S: And how is impurity threefold by way of mind? | T: Unskillful Mental Action “And how is one made impure in three ways by mental action?
an10.176:14.2 | S: It’s when someone is covetous. They covet the wealth and belongings of others: ‘Oh, if only their belongings were mine!’ | T: There is the case where a certain person is covetous. He covets the belongings of others, thinking, ‘O, that what belongs to others would be mine!’
an10.176:15.1 | S: They have ill will and malicious intentions: ‘May these sentient beings be killed, slaughtered, slain, destroyed, or annihilated!’ | T: He bears ill will, corrupt in the resolves of his heart: ‘May these beings be killed or cut apart or crushed or destroyed, or may they not exist at all!’
an10.176:16.1 | S: They have wrong view. Their perspective is distorted: | T: He has wrong view, is warped in the way he sees things:
an10.176:16.2 | S: ‘There’s no meaning in giving, sacrifice, or offerings. There’s no fruit or result of good and bad deeds. There’s no afterlife. There’s no such thing as mother and father, or beings that are reborn spontaneously. And there’s no ascetic or brahmin who is rightly comported and rightly practiced, and who describes the afterlife after realizing it with their own insight.’ | T: ‘There is nothing given, nothing offered, nothing sacrificed. There is no fruit or result of good or bad actions. There is no this world, no next world, no mother, no father, no spontaneously reborn beings; no contemplatives or brahmans who, faring rightly & practicing rightly, proclaim this world & the next after having directly known & realized it for themselves.’
an10.176:16.3 | S: This is the threefold impurity by way of mind. | T: This is how one is made impure in three ways by mental action.
an10.176:17.1 | S: These are the ten ways of doing unskillful deeds. | T: “These, Cunda, are the ten courses of unskillful action.
an10.176:17.2 | S: When you have these ten ways of doing unskillful deeds, then if you rise early, whether or not you stroke the earth from your bed, you’re still impure. | T: When a person is endowed with these ten courses of unskillful action, then even if he gets up at the proper time from his bed and touches the earth, he is still impure. If he doesn’t touch the earth, he is still impure.
an10.176:18.1 | S: Whether or not you stroke fresh cow dung, you’re still impure. | T: If he touches wet cow dung, he is still impure. If he doesn’t touch wet cow dung, he is still impure.
an10.176:19.1 | S: Whether or not you stroke green grass, you’re still impure. | T: If he touches green grass… If he doesn’t touch green grass…
an10.176:20.1 | S: Whether or not you serve the sacred flame, you’re still impure. | T: If he worships a fire… If he doesn’t worship a fire…
an10.176:21.1 | S: Whether or not you revere the sun with cupped palms, you’re still impure. | T: If he pays homage to the sun with clasped hands… If he doesn’t pay homage to the sun with clasped hands…
an10.176:22.1 | S: Whether or not you immerse yourself in water three times, you’re still impure. | T: If he goes down into the water three times by nightfall… If he doesn’t go down into the water three times by nightfall, he is still impure.
an10.176:22.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an10.176:22.3 | S: These ten ways of doing unskillful deeds are impure and make things impure. | T: Because these ten courses of unskillful action are impure and cause impurity.
an10.176:23.1 | S: It’s because of those who do these ten kinds of unskillful deeds that hell, the animal realm, the ghost realm, or any other bad places are found. | T: And further, as a result of being endowed with these ten courses of unskillful action, (rebirth in) hell is declared, (rebirth in) an animal womb is declared, (rebirth in) the realm of hungry ghosts is declared—that or any other bad destination.
an10.176:24.1 | S: Cunda, purity is threefold by way of body, | T: “Now, Cunda, there are three ways in which one is made pure by bodily action,
an10.176:24.2 | S: fourfold by way of speech, | T: four ways in which one is made pure by verbal action,
an10.176:24.3 | S: and threefold by way of mind. | T: and three ways in which one is made pure by mental action.
an10.176:25.1 | S: And how is purity threefold by way of body? | T: Skillful Bodily Action “And how is one made pure in three ways by bodily action?
an10.176:25.2 | S: It’s when someone gives up killing living creatures, renouncing the rod and the sword. They’re scrupulous and kind, living full of sympathy for all living beings. | T: There is the case where a certain person, abandoning the taking of life, abstains from the taking of life. He dwells with his rod laid down, his knife laid down, scrupulous, merciful, compassionate for the welfare of all living beings.
an10.176:26.1 | S: They give up stealing. They don’t, with the intention to commit theft, take the wealth or belongings of others from village or wilderness. | T: Abandoning the taking of what is not given, he abstains from taking what is not given. He does not take, in the manner of a thief, things in a village or a wilderness that belong to others and have not been given by them.
an10.176:27.1 | S: They give up sexual misconduct. They don’t have relations with women who have their mother, father, both mother and father, brother, sister, relatives, or clan as guardian. They don’t have relations with a woman who is protected on principle, or who has a husband, or whose violation is punishable by law, or even one who has been garlanded as a token of betrothal. | T: Abandoning sexual misconduct, he abstains from sexual misconduct. He does not get sexually involved with those who are protected by their mothers, their fathers, their brothers, their sisters, their relatives, or their Dhamma; those with husbands, those who entail punishments, or even those crowned with flowers by another man.
an10.176:28.1 | S: This is the threefold purity by way of body. | T: This is how one is made pure in three ways by bodily action.
an10.176:29.1 | S: And how is purity fourfold by way of speech? | T: Skillful Verbal Action “And how is one made pure in four ways by verbal action?
an10.176:29.2 | S: It’s when someone gives up lying. They’re summoned to a council, an assembly, a family meeting, a guild, or to the royal court, and asked to bear witness: ‘Please, my friend, say what you know.’ Not knowing, they say ‘I don’t know.’ Knowing, they say ‘I know.’ Not seeing, they say ‘I don’t see.’ And seeing, they say ‘I see.’ So they don’t deliberately lie for the sake of themselves or another, or for some trivial worldly reason. | T: There is the case where a certain person, abandoning the telling of lies, abstains from telling lies. When he has been called to a town meeting, a group meeting, a gathering of his relatives, his guild, or of the royalty, if he is asked as a witness, ‘Come & tell, good man, what you know’: If he doesn’t know, he says, ‘I don’t know.’ If he does know, he says, ‘I know.’ If he hasn’t seen, he says, ‘I haven’t seen.’ If he has seen, he says, ’I have seen.’ Thus he doesn’t consciously tell a lie for his own sake, for the sake of another, or for the sake of any reward.
an10.176:30.1 | S: They give up backbiting. They don’t repeat in one place what they heard in another so as to divide people against each other. Instead, they reconcile those who are divided and support those who are united, delighting in harmony, loving harmony, speaking words that promote harmony. | T: Abandoning divisive speech, he abstains from divisive speech. What he has heard here he does not tell there to break those people apart from these people here. What he has heard there he does not tell here to break these people apart from those people there. Thus reconciling those who have broken apart or cementing those who are united, he loves concord, delights in concord, enjoys concord, speaks things that create concord.
an10.176:31.1 | S: They give up harsh speech. They speak in a way that’s mellow, pleasing to the ear, endearing, going to the heart, polite, and likable and agreeable to the people. | T: Abandoning harsh speech, he abstains from harsh speech. He speaks words that are soothing, pleasing to the ear, that are affectionate, that go to the heart, that are polite, appealing & pleasing to people at large.
an10.176:32.1 | S: They give up talking nonsense. Their words are timely, true, and meaningful, in line with the teaching and training. | T: Abandoning idle chatter, he abstains from idle chatter. He speaks in season, speaks what is based in fact, what is in accordance with the goal, the Dhamma, & the Vinaya.
an10.176:32.2 | S: They say things at the right time which are valuable, reasonable, succinct, and beneficial. | T: He speaks words worth treasuring, timely, reasonable, circumscribed, connected with the goal.
an10.176:33.1 | S: This is the fourfold purity by way of speech. | T: This is how one is made pure in four ways by verbal action.
an10.176:34.1 | S: And how is purity threefold by way of mind? | T: Skillful Mental Action “And how is one made pure in three ways by mental action?
an10.176:34.2 | S: It’s when someone is content. They don’t covet the wealth and belongings of others: ‘Oh, if only their belongings were mine!’ | T: There is the case where a certain person is not covetous. He does not covet the belongings of others, thinking, ‘O, that what belongs to others would be mine!’
an10.176:35.1 | S: They have a kind heart and loving intentions: ‘May these sentient beings live free of enmity and ill will, untroubled and happy!’ | T: He bears no ill will and is not corrupt in the resolves of his heart. (He thinks,) ‘May these beings be free from animosity, free from oppression, free from trouble, and may they look after themselves with ease!’
an10.176:36.1 | S: They have right view, an undistorted perspective: | T: He has right view and is not warped in the way he sees things:
an10.176:36.2 | S: ‘There is meaning in giving, sacrifice, and offerings. There are fruits and results of good and bad deeds. There is an afterlife. There are such things as mother and father, and beings that are reborn spontaneously. And there are ascetics and brahmins who are rightly comported and rightly practiced, and who describe the afterlife after realizing it with their own insight.’ | T: ‘There is what is given, what is offered, what is sacrificed. There are fruits & results of good & bad actions. There is this world & the next world. There is mother & father. There are spontaneously reborn beings; there are contemplatives & brahmans who, faring rightly & practicing rightly, proclaim this world & the next after having directly known & realized it for themselves.’
an10.176:37.1 | S: This is the threefold purity by way of mind. | T: This is how one is made pure in three ways by mental action.
an10.176:38.1 | S: These are the ten ways of doing skillful deeds. | T: “These, Cunda, are the ten courses of skillful action.
an10.176:38.2 | S: When you have these ten ways of doing skillful deeds, then if you rise early, whether or not you stroke the earth from your bed, you’re still pure. | T: When a person is endowed with these ten courses of skillful action, then even if he gets up at the proper time from his bed and touches the earth, he is still pure. If he doesn’t touch the earth, he is still pure.
an10.176:39.1 | S: Whether or not you stroke fresh cow dung, you’re still pure. | T: If he touches wet cow dung, he is still pure. If he doesn’t touch wet cow dung, he is still pure.
an10.176:40.1 | S: Whether or not you stroke green grass, you’re still pure. | T: If he touches green grass… If he doesn’t touch green grass…
an10.176:41.1 | S: Whether or not you serve the sacred flame, you’re still pure. | T: If he worships a fire… If he doesn’t worship a fire…
an10.176:42.1 | S: Whether or not you revere the sun with cupped palms, you’re still pure. | T: If he pays homage to the sun with clasped hands… If he doesn’t pay homage to the sun with clasped hands…
an10.176:43.1 | S: Whether or not you immerse yourself in water three times, you’re still pure. | T: If he goes down into the water three times by nightfall… If he doesn’t go down into the water three times by nightfall, he is still pure.
an10.176:43.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an10.176:43.3 | S: These ten ways of doing skillful deeds are pure and make things pure. | T: Because these ten courses of skillful action are pure and cause purity.
an10.176:44.1 | S: It’s because of those who do these ten kinds of skillful deeds that gods, humans, or any other good places are found.” | T: And further, as a result of being endowed with these ten courses of skillful action, (rebirth among) the devas is declared, (rebirth among) human beings is declared—that or any other good destination.”
an10.176:45.1 | S: When he said this, Cunda the smith said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Cunda the silversmith said to the Blessed One:
an10.176:45.2 | S: “Excellent, sir! Excellent! … | T: “Magnificent, venerable sir! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has the Blessed One—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear. I go to the Blessed One for refuge, to the Dhamma, and to the Saṅgha of monks.
an10.176:45.3 | S: From this day forth, may the Buddha remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: May the Blessed One remember me as a lay follower who has gone to him for refuge, from this day forward, for life.”
an10.176:45.4 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)


